Why they are here rather than linked: requirement S-01 obliges this project to
cite the exact draft revision it was written against, and U-15 turns that into
pinning a copy. Each of these files says of itself that it "may be updated,
replaced, or obsoleted by other documents at any time", and both carry an expiry
-- 3 September 2026 for OAuth 2.1, 7 January 2027 for CIMD. A URL at ietf.org
serves whatever revision is current, so a link is not a citation of the text the
requirement ids were written against. The file here is.

Nothing under src/, hosts/, testing/ or tests/ contains a Code Component lifted
from either draft. If one is ever taken, TLP section 4.e attaches the Revised BSD
text to that copy, and it is named here in the same commit.

One further file is adapted from third-party text and is likewise NOT covered by
this repository's Apache-2.0 licence:

  CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md

  Adapted from the Contributor Covenant, version 3.0, permanently available at
  https://www.contributor-covenant.org/version/3/0/ and fetched 2026-08-24.

  Contributor Covenant is stewarded by the Organization for Ethical Source and
  licensed under the Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International
  licence (CC BY-SA 4.0), https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/.

  Because that file is an adaptation rather than a verbatim copy -- the reporting
  channel is this project's, and one passage is rewritten to say that reports
  reach one person rather than a moderation team -- ShareAlike attaches, so the
  file carries CC BY-SA 4.0 and says so in its own last section. Two consequences
  worth stating rather than leaving to be worked out: a downstream fork may reuse
  it under those terms and not under Apache-2.0, and an edit to it is an edit to
  a CC BY-SA 4.0 work.

  Nothing under src/, hosts/, testing/ or tests/ derives from it. It is prose
  about conduct and shares no text with any file that compiles.

This file describes the contents of the repository. The published packages
contain none of the third-party material above -- they carry compiled code from
src/ and testing/ only, plus README.md, which is this project's own -- so their
licence metadata is Apache-2.0 with nothing further attached.

spec/README.md carries the same statement in prose, with the reasoning.
